    "md": "# NodeBlog — Hack The Box (Easy)\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nFirst, we check that we have connectivity with the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/01.png)\n\nNow we run a port scan to find the open ports and services on the server.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/02.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/03.png)\n\nWe see port 5000; it's commonly used for Flask applications, a framework written in Python. Before visiting the website, let's check which technologies it uses with `whatweb`.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/04.png)\n\nNot much there either, so let's visit the page. We find a login panel.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/05.png)\n\n---\n\n## User enumeration and NoSQL Injection\n\nWe can enumerate users; the user `admin` exists.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/06.png)\n\nI tried several SQLi payloads and, after a while, found NoSQL payloads on HackTricks. I'm going to try them, but first the data needs to be sent as JSON, so I'll change the `Content-Type` to see if that's possible. A basic injection from my notes worked: we're in.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/07.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/08.png)\n\nWe use the generated cookie so we can work from the browser. This is possible because `HttpOnly` is set to `false`. When trying to upload a shell we get an XML error, which suggests we should try XXE.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/09.png)\n\n---\n\n## XXE / LFI in the file upload\n\nLooking at the history in Burp Suite, we can see the expected structure of the XML file to upload. Let's try to read an internal file, that is, an LFI through the XXE.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/10.png)\n\nHere's the file; let's upload it. The markdown field should show `/etc/passwd`.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/11.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/12.png)\n\nAnd it worked. Now that we can read files, after thinking for a while I decided to look at the server's own files, that is, the website's code. Since it runs on Node.js, I looked up how those files are usually named, but first we need their path. One way to get it is to force errors and see which directory they're in.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/13.png)\n\nI managed to force an error; the path is `/opt/blog`.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/14.png)\n\nAnd it works: we can read `server.js`.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/15.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/16.png)\n\n---\n\n## Insecure deserialization → RCE\n\nSince I don't know much JavaScript yet, I gave the code to an AI to look for possible flaws. Of the issues it pointed out, the most important one is the insecure deserialization.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/17.png)\n\nHackTricks has this payload; we just need to adapt it, replacing the sample one with our cookie.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/18.png)\n\nThis is how it looks; let's test it with a `ping`.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/19.png)\n\nNow we listen for ICMP traffic with `tcpdump` and send the request from Burp Suite with the modified cookie.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/20.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/21.png)\n\nSince it works, I'll try sending myself a basic bash reverse shell. For it to work it has to be URL-encoded, because base64 characters and characters such as pipes can cause problems. I also removed the quotes from the `echo`, because it wasn't working; it was probably breaking when decoding. But we have the shell.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/22.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/23.png)\n\n---\n\n## Post-exploitation: first flag\n\nWe can't access the folder, but since we own it, we can change its permissions.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/24.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/25.png)\n\nWe have the first flag; now we need to escalate privileges.\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation: MongoDB\n\nWhile enumerating, we find we have access to the database, so we could get in and look for our user's password. `mongosh` didn't work, but I found we can use the older shell, `mongo`.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/26.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/27.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/28.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/29.png)\n\nWe find the `admin` user in the blog database; the other databases had nothing interesting. The password is being reused.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/30.png)\n\nAnd since we can run any command with `su`, we become root. And that's it: *pwned*.\n\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/31.png)\n![](img/htb-nodeblog/32.png)\n"
