    "summary": "File-upload foothold with a double extension bypassing an Apache misconfig, command injection in a cron script via a filename, then root through a sudo network script.",
    "md": "# Networked — Hack The Box (Easy)\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nThe first thing we do is check that we have connectivity with the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/01.png)\n\nOnce confirmed, we scan the open ports and services.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/02.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/03.png)\n\nWe see there's a website. Before visiting it, let's check which technologies it uses with `whatweb`.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/04.png)\n\nIt runs PHP, and quite an old version.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/05.png)\n\n---\n\n## Web enumeration\n\nThe website shows a message. In the source code I found something interesting: it looks like there should be an `upload` directory and a `gallery` one. Let's see if we can access them directly.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/06.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/07.png)\n\nWe can't access them.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/08.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/09.png)\n\nLet's fuzz for files and directories. There's `uploads`, not `upload`.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/10.png)\n\nWe'll go there, but first to another path that showed up: `backup`. In it we find the source code of `upload.php`, which checks that only jpg, png... images are uploaded. Let's see if we can bypass this validation.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/11.png)\n\n---\n\n## File upload bypass\n\nWe create our reverse shell with a modified extension.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/12.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/13.png)\n\nIt didn't work. Looking more closely at the code, I see the check is done in a file called `lib.php`, which uses `mime` to check the file's content type.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/14.png)\n\nAfter some searching, I found this way to bypass the check: [bypass](https://dev.to/giannisftaras/file-upload-bypass-using-mime-type-4ej0).\n\n![](img/htb-networked/15.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/16.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/17.png)\n\nBasically, I used the shell I already had, but added the `.gif` extension at the beginning.\n\n---\n\n## RCE via command injection\n\nIn `guly`'s home we see that, in the second `exec`, the value of `$value` is concatenated inside a function without being sanitized, so if we break out with a `;` we should be able to run commands. Gemini helped me understand this.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/18.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/19.png)\n\nNow we just have to wait. This shell didn't work, so I tried another one.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/20.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/21.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/22.png)\n\nI took this shell from the writeup, because none of the ones I had tried worked. `penelope` didn't work well either; I tried a plain `nc` listener and it worked.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/23.png)\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation\n\nRunning `sudo -l`, we see we can run a script as root without a password.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/24.png)\n![](img/htb-networked/25.png)\n\nLooking at the code, it runs as root and the regex allows `/`. So if we spawn a shell when it asks for a value, we should get a root shell. Since spaces are allowed, we can't break out with `;` or `|`, but if we type a space followed by the shell, what it ends up executing is `/bin/bash`.\n\n![](img/htb-networked/26.png)\n"
