    "summary": "WordPress chain: BookingPress SQLi (CVE-2022-0739) for the admin hash, media-upload XXE (CVE-2021-29447) to leak FTP creds, and root by cracking the Passpie PGP key with john.",
    "md": "# MetaTwo — Hack The Box (Easy)\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nFirst, we check that we have connectivity with the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/01.png)\n\nOnce confirmed, we enumerate the possible open ports.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/02.png)\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/03.png)\n\nWe can see a website, SSH and an FTP service. In the second scan, the FTP isn't reported as allowing anonymous access, so let's go to the website.\n\nTo access the website we need to add the domain to our `/etc/hosts`.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/04.png)\n\nThis is what we see when we open the website.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/05.png)\n\nIf we click the link they give us, we can see it's a WordPress, and the login panel is at the default path.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/06.png)\n\n---\n\n## WordPress enumeration\n\nLet's check the WordPress version, the plugins, and whether we can get a user for the login panel.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/07.png)\n\nWe see that `xmlrpc` is enabled and that the WordPress version is 5.6.2, quite old.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/08.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/09.png)\n\nWhile the scan finished, I was browsing the site, and in the source code of the events page we see quite a few WordPress plugins in use.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/10.png)\n\nAnd we find an interesting one: `bookingpress`, version 1.0.10.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/11.png)\n\n`wpscan` was taking a long time and I realized it was too specific, so I ran a more generic scan.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/12.png)\n\nWe see that the plugin I mentioned earlier has a total of 16 vulnerabilities.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/13.png)\n\nOf all of them, the one that caught my eye the most is an unauthenticated SQL injection. It explains that this plugin fails to sanitize the data sent by the user via POST before it's used to dynamically build the SQL query, through the AJAX action `bookingpress_front_get_category_services`.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/14.png)\n\nWe also found the users `admin` and `manager`.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/15.png)\n\n---\n\n## Exploitation: SQL Injection in BookingPress\n\nThe exploit tells us to make a request with the `wpnonce` set to the value on the website, so we grab it from the source code.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/16.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/17.png)\n\nAnd as we can see, it works: here we can see the database version. To make the injection easier, based on what I saw in the writeup, we can use SQLMap to run the injection on the request. For this we just pass the request to Burp Suite, save it from the Repeater to a file, and use it with SQLMap.\n\nWe pass the request to Burp Suite.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/18.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/19.png)\n\nWe save it.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/20.png)\n\nAnd we run SQLMap with the request.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/21.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/22.png)\n\nWe see the database in use is `blog`.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/23.png)\n\nAnother option would be to brute-force the `XMLRPC` with `wpscan`, but it will be slower than the SQL injection. Still, it's worth a try.\n\nAfter a while, the table that caught my eye the most was `wp_users`. Let's look at its columns.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/24.png)\n\nWe can see the password and username columns.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/25.png)\n\nNow let's dump these values.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/26.png)\n\nIt returns them blank, but tells us that adding the `--hex` or `--no-cast` switch will work. I used `--hex` and it worked correctly.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/27.png)\n\nThe hash type is `phpass`.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/28.png)\n\nWe have the first password. Let's set it aside while we see what we can do with it and whether it's reused anywhere.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/29.png)\n\n---\n\n## Access to the WordPress panel\n\nIt works for logging into the WordPress panel.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/30.png)\n\nWe can't log in over FTP.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/31.png)\n\nAnd not over SSH either.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/32.png)\n\nI also don't see a place where, through the template, I could spawn a shell or anything like that. Earlier I saw that the version had many vulnerabilities, so I'll check whether any could be useful.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/33.png)\n\n---\n\n## XXE in the media upload (CVE-2021-29447)\n\nThe vulnerability with the highest severity is this one. It's an XXE.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/34.png)\n\nLooking for a PoC, I found this one, which explains it quite well and is a condensed way to perform the XXE.\n\nhttps://github.com/motikan2010/CVE-2021-29447\n\nAlthough I preferred to do it the way shown on the wpscan page.\n\nhttps://wpscan.com/vulnerability/cbbe6c17-b24e-4be4-8937-c78472a138b5/\n\nWe simply create two files: one that will pass the result of the DTD to our server, and then the DTD itself. The WordPress one didn't work for me; checking the writeup, I used the `evil.dtd` they provide and it worked. I think it was because of the way it was base64-encoded.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/35.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/36.png)\n\nAnd here we have the `passwd` file. We see a new user called `jnelson`, but we don't have their credentials. A typical place where credentials are stored is `wp-config.php`. Let's try to exfiltrate its contents.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/37.png)\n\nAnd here it is. Let's decode it.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/38.png)\n\nWe get the WordPress database password.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/39.png)\n\nWe find a username and password for FTP.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/40.png)\n\n---\n\n## FTP access\n\nWe're inside the FTP. Let's see what we have.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/41.png)\n\nIn `blog` we have the whole WordPress configuration.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/42.png)\n\nAnd in `mailer` we have a folder and a file.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/43.png)\n\nThe PHP script in `mailer` gives us the email and password of the user we found in `/etc/passwd`, `jnelson`.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/44.png)\n\n---\n\n## Lateral movement: SSH as jnelson\n\nLet's see whether the credentials are reused and whether they work for SSH.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/45.png)\n\nThe MySQL service is open.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/46.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/47.png)\n\nLet's try connecting with these credentials. We're in; let's see what's in this database.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/48.png)\n\nOn second thought, this is the WordPress database I already mentioned, so it won't help me. Since I couldn't find where to go next, I checked the writeup: there's a program called `passpie`, which exports in plain text the credentials stored in it. Since we don't have the password, we can head to the directory where `passpie` stores those passwords.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/49.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/50.png)\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation: passpie\n\nIn this directory we find several valuable things, but the one that catches our eye the most is `.keys`, where the public and private keys are stored. They're protected, so we'll try brute-forcing to see if we can get the password.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/51.png)\n\nI split the keys into two files, one with the public key and one with the private key.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/52.png)\n\nNow it's time to crack the private one and see if it's possible.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/53.png)\n\nAnd we have the password for `passpie`. And with it, root.\n\n![](img/htb-metatwo/54.png)\n![](img/htb-metatwo/55.png)\n"
